    "result_text": "The full build passes. The implementation is complete and green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe task asked for a `riskFlag` on the owner read-model that is `true` when **any** of three soft-risk signals holds. I implemented it as a derived response field (consistent across the POST create response and later GETs), matching how the codebase already derives `ownerSegment`, `contactPreference`, etc.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `riskFlag` boolean (readOnly) to the Owner schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field and getter/setter.\n\n2. **`DisposableEmailDomains.java`** \u2014 made the helper `public` and added `isDisposableAdjacent(String email)`. Since exactly-blocked disposable domains are rejected at create time (400) and never persist, \"disposable-adjacent\" captures the near-misses: a domain sharing a blocked provider's second-level name under a different TLD (e.g. `mailinator.net`) or carrying a throwaway-mail token (`tempmail`, `guerrilla`, `throwaway`, `disposable`, \u2026). Blank/domain-less emails are never adjacent.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `riskFlag` mapping backed by a `riskFlag(owner)` default method:\n   - possible duplicate \u2192 `owner.getPossibleDuplicate()`\n   - disposable-adjacent email \u2192 `DisposableEmailDomains.isDisposableAdjacent(owner.getEmail())`\n   - city over soft capacity \u2192 `owner.getCapacityWarning()` (the 40\u201349 warning band, i.e. over the soft threshold before the hard cap of 50)\n   \n   `true` if any hold, else `false`.\n\nTest results: `AcceptanceTest.coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` passes (`riskFlag == false` for a normal structured owner, which has no email, no duplicate, and an empty city), and the full suite is `BUILD SUCCESS` (1 test, 0 failures).",
